+every database/backup/upgrade/rollback path are byte-identical):
+  the indexd pid file records the process IDENTITY, not just a pid: "<pid> <starttime> <boot_id>" (starttime = field 22
+  of /proc/<pid>/stat, written at Popen time while the child cannot yet be reaped, so the triple is exact). A pid whose
+  live process has another starttime/boot is a reused pid: it is never signalled and our indexd counts as exited. A
+  zombie counts as exited. stop/crash-indexd bind a pidfd, RE-VERIFY the identity through it, and signal and wait on
+  that pidfd only, so a pid reused between check and signal cannot be hit. start refuses while the identified indexd
+  still runs (it would orphan it). A pid file that cannot be parsed, or a /proc read that fails for any reason other
+  than "no such process", is an UNKNOWN state: an error, never "stopped". Before signalling, the identified process
+  must also be in the controller's OWN cgroup (the fixture's queue scope) and must not be one of its ancestors;
+  otherwise the controller refuses (an error), never signals.
+
 Nothing here activates anything by itself: every mutating command needs an explicit --confirm flag naming the
 action, and production profiles additionally refuse to run unless the node's ALLOW file exists (the same pattern as
